  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Honest Results Page",
    "overview": "This is the travel-results page everyone already knows from Booking, Airbnb and Kayak, kept deliberately familiar and executed with more care. A solid sea-teal header carries the brand and season tabs; a white summary bar restates the search; a filter rail sits left and hotel rows stack in the main column. Nothing about the layout asks to be learned. The craft goes into the price block, where every number comes with its qualifier and a price-insight strip instead of an unexplained \"was\" badge.\n\nThe ground is white and cool off-white, light only. Teal is the colour of action and selection; coral is reserved for what an agency claims and what is about to run out; sand marks the middle of the price-insight bar. Density is moderate: laptop-first rows that show photo, fit checklist and price at once, collapsing to a single column on a phone.\n\nHonesty states are visual citizens, not footnotes: hollow rings for unverified answers, dashed rings for unknowns, dashed \"ask\" chips, and amber qualifiers for 2-adult prices all use the same system tokens as everything else.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Category-standard results layout: teal header, summary bar, 288px filter rail, hotel rows.",
      "White surfaces on a cool off-white page, separated by hairlines and two soft shadows.",
      "One typeface (Onest) for everything, tabular figures on by default.",
      "Pills for anything you toggle; 8/12/16px corners for things you press, frame or read.",
      "One authored inline SVG icon set at 1.75 stroke."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Claim Colour Rule",
        "body": "Coral is only for what an agency claims (discounts, campaigns) and what runs out (deadlines), plus the shortlisted heart. It never marks a button, a link, or a selection.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Teal Means You Can Act Rule",
        "body": "Sea teal fills only things you can press or have selected: the header, primary buttons, selected segments, form accents, focus. Body text is never teal; links and names are deep teal.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Weight Does It Rule",
        "body": "Hierarchy comes from weight and size within Onest; there is no second typeface and no uppercase label style.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Figures Line Up Rule",
        "body": "Tabular figures stay on globally (`font-feature-settings: \"tnum\" 1` on body); never switch prices to proportional figures.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lift on Hover Rule",
        "body": "Rows rest at the Rest shadow and move to Lifted on hover (with a stronger hairline), over 0.2s ease. Nothing else changes elevation on interaction.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep every price beside its qualifier (who it covers, board, nights, source); a 2-adult price shows its qualifier in warn 600.",
      "**Do** use sea teal for press-and-select only, and deep teal for links and hotel names.",
      "**Do** draw icons from the inline SVG sprite: 24px viewBox, 1.75 stroke, round caps and joins, currentColor, 18px by default.",
      "**Do** mark unknowns with dashed outlines and unverified answers with hollow rings, using the same semantic tokens as confirmed states.",
      "**Do** keep elevation to the Rest and Lifted shadows, lifting rows on hover.",
      "**Do** honour reduced motion: transitions switch off under `prefers-reduced-motion`."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use coral for buttons, links or selection; it belongs to claims, deadlines and the shortlisted heart.",
      "**Don't** add a dark theme; the product is light only by the owner's choice.",
      "**Don't** introduce a second typeface or proportional figures for prices.",
      "**Don't** use hard, unblurred offset shadows; depth here is soft and ink-tinted.",
      "**Don't** show a \"was\" price or discount without the insight strip and verdict that say whether it is real."
    ]
  }
}